<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sokoto Caliphate</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Sokoto</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Sokoto <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">Caliphate</a> was an Islamic state in what is now northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a>, founded through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jihad">jihad</a> led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/usman-dan-fodio">Usman dan Fodio</a> from 1804. It united <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hausa">Hausa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fula">Fulani</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirates</a> under the caliph at Sokoto and became one of the largest states in nineteenth-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>. It was conquered by the British in the early twentieth century, and accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oyo-empire">Oyo Empire</a> note its role in Oyo's decline.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sokoto-caliphate.jpg" alt="Sokoto Caliphate" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Orange Tuesday · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Sokoto_Caliphate.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The court titles, tax systems, walled cities and markets of the Hausa states were largely taken over by the <b>Sokoto Caliphate</b>, whose Fulani emirs ruled through the institutions they had overthrown, and many of them survive in the emirates of northern Nigeria, such as Kano, Katsina and Zaria.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hausa-kingdoms">Hausa kingdoms · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>…and the Oyo people lost the northern portion of their region, refugees poured into Ibadan, and when the <b>Sokoto Caliphate</b> attempted to expand further into the south of what is now Nigeria it was decisively defeated by the armies of Ibadan in 1840, which eventually halted its progress.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ibadan">Ibadan · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>A young imam, Sulaimanu, became the first Fulani emir, and Kano became an emirate of the <b>Sokoto Caliphate</b> until the British took the city in February 1903.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-kano">Kingdom of Kano · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Around 1817 Afonja, based at Ilorin, rebelled openly and enlisted the support of the Fulani Muslim cleric Alimi and Hausa and Fulani followers inspired by the <b>Sokoto</b> jihad, which brought a dangerous new enemy into the centre of the empire.</blockquote><a href="/empire/oyo-empire">Oyo Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>In the 18th century the Tuareg scholar Jibril ibn ’Umar preached revolutionary jihad, and his student Usman dan Fodio went on to lead the <b>Sokoto</b> jihads and found the Sokoto Caliphate.</blockquote><a href="/culture/tuareg">Tuareg · Beliefs &amp; Worldview</a></li><li><blockquote>…Lugard in The Dual Mandate in British Tropical Africa (1922), existing rulers such as the emirs of the <b>Sokoto Caliphate</b> in Northern Nigeria kept their courts and taxes under British supervision, as the Indian princely states did under the Raj; where no suitable ruler existed, administrators appointed “warrant…</blockquote><a href="/government/colonial-rule">Colonial rule · How It Works</a></li></ul></section>
